    1: //! Wire-supplied path validation and safe joining.
    2: //!
    3: //! Every place blit accepts a path string from the wire (push manifest
    4: //! entries, pull file headers, tar shard inner paths, resume block
    5: //! identifiers, purge requests) must route through one of the helpers
    6: //! here. The shared chokepoint replaces a half-dozen ad-hoc validators
    7: //! that drifted apart and missed escape forms.
    8: //!
    9: //! A buggy or malicious peer should not be able to write outside the
   10: //! destination root by sending:
   11: //!   - absolute paths (`/etc/passwd`)
   12: //!   - parent-dir traversal (`../../etc/passwd`, `foo/../../etc/passwd`)
   13: //!   - Windows drive prefixes (`C:\evil`), UNC paths
   14: //!     (`\\?\C:\evil`, `\\server\share\evil`), or single-leading-
   15: //!     backslash Windows-root forms (`\Windows\System32`)
   16: //!   - embedded NUL bytes
   17: //!   - `"."` / `"./"` / `"./."` (these normalize to empty but are not
   18: //!     the legitimate single-file empty-path case)
   19: //!
   20: //! Per F1 of `docs/reviews/codebase_review_2026-05-01.md`. Path safety is
   21: //! sequenced first in `docs/plan/PIPELINE_UNIFICATION.md` because every
   22: //! receive sink — current `pull_sync.rs` custom path AND the unified
   23: //! pipeline — needs it.
   24: //!
   25: //! ─── Contract: two layers of safety ──────────────────────────────
   26: //!
   27: //! `safe_join(root, wire)` performs a *lexical* containment check.
   28: //! It rejects path strings that escape via syntax (above), but does
   29: //! NOT canonicalize the result or resolve symlinks.
   30: //!
   31: //! `contained_join(canonical_root, wire)` adds the canonicalize-
   32: //! and-check pass on top of `safe_join`'s lexical layer. It walks
   33: //! to the deepest existing ancestor of the target, canonicalizes,
   34: //! and confirms the resolution stays under `canonical_root`. Use it
   35: //! at every daemon site that touches a path under a module root
   36: //! (F2 of `docs/reviews/codebase_review_2026-05-01.md`).
   37: //!
   38: //! Both layers are needed:
   39: //!
   40: //!   - `safe_join` alone fails on `module/link/file` where `link` is
   41: //!     a symlink to `/etc`. Lexically the wire path is fine; the
   42: //!     filesystem write follows the symlink outside the module.
   43: //!     `contained_join` rejects this case via canonicalize.
   44: //!
   45: //!   - `contained_join` alone is more permissive about wire syntax
   46: //!     than `safe_join` would be. Always run the wire input through
   47: //!     `validate_wire_path` (which `contained_join` does internally
   48: //!     via `safe_join`) so absolute / Windows-shaped / NUL inputs
   49: //!     are rejected before any filesystem call.
   50: //!
   51: //! TOCTOU note: `contained_join` is check-then-use, so a symlink
   52: //! could in principle be swapped between the canonicalize call and
   53: //! the actual fs op. For the trust model (authenticated peers,
   54: //! operator-controlled module roots) this matches rsync's chroot
   55: //! module behavior. A fully race-proof alternative would use
   56: //! `openat` + `O_NOFOLLOW` per-component descent.
   57: 
