   33: // Case-sensitivity is a property of the platform's default filesystem, and
   34: // this key exists to model it so a mirror never deletes a file the source
   35: // kept under a different case. Windows (NTFS) and macOS (APFS) are
   36: // case-insensitive by default → fold (posix-normalize + ASCII-lowercase);
   37: // Linux (ext4/xfs) is case-sensitive → exact. This matters most for the
   38: // unified session, the first mirror path that diffs a WIRE source set (the
   39: // source's on-disk case) against the DEST filesystem: on a case-insensitive
   40: // dest the two cases can diverge, and an exact key would delete the
   41: // just-written file (review otp-6b F1). Case-insensitive Linux mounts and
   42: // case-sensitive macOS volumes are rare misconfigurations a compile-time cfg
   43: // cannot detect; ASCII-only folding leaves Unicode case variants approximate
   44: // (a known gap, same as the pre-existing Windows behavior).
